Ladybird now treats a symbol like punctuation in word movement, and
crosses a run of punctuation and symbols in one step. Blink does the
same: Its IsWordBoundary() in position_units_word.cc takes any character
that unicode::IsPunct() or unicode::IsSymbol() accepts. Gecko nearly
does: IsPunctuationForWordSelect() in nsUnicharUtils.cpp counts the Sc,
Sm, and So categories as punctuation, and leaves out only Sk. WebKit
stops at each symbol, and fails move-by-word-with-symbol.html.

Run in Chrome 158, Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5, in a contenteditable, a
word step forward from the space before “👍👍👍” lands after the last emoji
in Chrome (as in Ladybird now), and after the first one in Firefox and
WebKit. Forward from the space before “,.;:+~”, Chrome stops after the
run (as Ladybird now does), Firefox crosses the space and the next word
too, and WebKit stops after “,.;:+”. In a textarea, Option+Right from
the start of “>>>>>>> word” lands at the end in Firefox and WebKit (as
in Ladybird, whose text controls also skip the separator after a run),
and after the run in Chrome. And Option+Backspace after “ab $$$$”
deletes all of the text in Firefox (as in Ladybird now), just the “$$$$”
in Chrome, and a single “$” in WebKit.
